#+TITLE: empty-drawing-decline-test-premise-fails-on-windows — the unwritable-location fixture is writable on Windows
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-27

* Observed

=test:alfe:windows= on the clautolisp 2.2.133 child (job 16762247996) is the
only red Windows unit lane, and it fails on exactly one check:

#+begin_example
Did 1308 checks.  Pass: 1307 (99%)  Fail: 1 ( 0%)
  AN-UNWRITABLE-DIRECTORY-DECLINES-RATHER-THAN-FAILS-THE-RUN
#+end_example

=test:clautolisp:windows= and =test:autolisp-test:windows= are both green in the
same child, so this is one test, not a broken toolchain.

* What the test needs, and why it is hard to get portably

=FRESH-EMPTY-DWG= is a *courtesy*: a backend that cannot get a drawing falls
back on template discovery, so the function must return NIL rather than signal
and take the run down. Testing that needs a location the write genuinely
CANNOT succeed in, on every platform — and that is the whole difficulty. Two
attempts so far, each defeated by Windows in a different way:

1. =/proc/definitely/not/writable/= — unwritable on Linux only. On Windows it
   maps to a perfectly creatable =C:\proc\...=, the write SUCCEEDED, and the
   lane went red.
2. A directory *below a regular file*, which cannot be created anywhere:
   ENOTDIR on POSIX, its equivalent on Windows. Still red.

*Attempt 2's premise was verified on POSIX and holds there*, which is what
narrows this down. Measured with uiop 3.3.7.3 on Linux:
=uiop:with-temporary-file (:pathname p)= leaves a real file at =p= during the
body, and =(ensure-directories-exist "<p>/sub/empty.dwg")= signals
=SB-INT:SIMPLE-FILE-ERROR=. So the POSIX side was never the problem and the
difference is on the Windows side of one of two things:

- the fixture — the temporary file may not exist as a file when the body runs;
  or
- *the pathname arithmetic* — =uiop:ensure-directory-pathname= on
  =C:/Temp/xyz.tmp= plus =merge-pathnames "sub/"= may not produce a path whose
  parent is that file. If it lands somewhere else, the write succeeds
  harmlessly and the test fails for a reason that has nothing to do with what
  it is testing.

The second is the likelier of the two and matches the shape of attempt 1.

* Done here (clautolisp 2.2.134): the test now says WHICH

It could not be reproduced from Linux, so instead of guessing at a fix the test
was made to *assert its premise* and report the paths. It now:

- builds the blocker file *explicitly* (=with-open-file=) rather than through a
  temporary-file macro — one less platform-dependent behaviour between the
  fixture and the thing under test;
- asserts the blocker exists AS A FILE, quoting =probe-file= when it does not;
- asserts the target sits *directly below* the blocker, comparing namestrings
  and quoting blocker, target and the target's parent when it does not — this
  is the check that names a pathname-arithmetic difference instead of leaving
  it as a mystery;
- reports what =FRESH-EMPTY-DWG= actually returned, and for which target.

Still 2 more checks and green on SBCL and CCL (1330 checks, 0 failures).

So the next Windows run answers the question by itself: either a premise check
fails and names the paths, or the decline check fails with the pathname it
managed to write into.

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The next =test:alfe:windows= identifies which of the two premises breaks,
      from its own failure message, without another investigation.
- [ ] The fixture is then made unwritable in a way that holds on Windows too,
      OR — if no portable unwritable location exists — the test declares
      =:skip-on-os (:windows …)= with the reason stated, per the convention for
      a scenario whose premise is false on a platform.
- [ ] =test:alfe:windows= green.

* Note

Carved out of =alfe-windows-drive-protocol-three-evals-fails= when that closed:
its own test now PASSES on Windows, and keeping it open for an unrelated red
test would have misreported what was fixed. A lane that is red for two
different reasons needs two tickets, or the second reason inherits the first
one's conclusion.

* CLOSED 2026-10-03 -- green on Windows since the 2.2.134 rework

The instrumented test was meant to fail on the next Windows run and name
the broken premise. It did not fail: AN-UNWRITABLE-DIRECTORY-DECLINES-
RATHER-THAN-FAILS-THE-RUN passes on test:alfe:windows (job 16912990831,
MR !376: 1378 checks, 0 failures; its 3 checks pass), and every
test:alfe:windows run in the job history today succeeded (7 of 7; the
others cancelled, none failed). So the cause was the FIXTURE premise --
the temporary-file macro's file was not a file when the body ran on
Windows -- and 2.2.134's explicit with-open-file blocker, written to fix
exactly that ambiguity, fixed it. No :skip-on-os was needed.
